{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-279719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x-4y=-20.$\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x-4y & =-20 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+12x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}+12x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-20 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+6x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-20 \\\\ 2[(x+3)^{2}-9]+[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =-20 \\\\ 2(x+3)^{2}-18+(y-2)^{2}-4 & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+3)^{2}-18+(y-2)^{2}-4 & =-20 \\\\ 2(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =-20+18+4 \\\\ 2(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{1}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sqrt{2}>1,$ the length of the major axis  $=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2},$ and the length of the minor axis  $=2\\cdot 1=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8fe20d1dde83b850c6707a517b045a868862fa76d815f8b06488be51ce798b91"} {"question" "q-279719" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279719" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279719" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279719" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-7632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is $5{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-10x-36y=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-7632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-10x-36y & =4 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}-10x)+(9{y}^{2}-36y) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10x)+(9{y}^{2}-36y) & =4 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2x]+9[{y}^{2}-4y] & =4 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+9[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =4 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-1]+9[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =4 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}-5+9(y-2)^{2}-36 & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x-1)^{2}-5+9(y-2)^{2}-36 & =4 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}+9(y-2)^{2} & =4+5+36 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}+9(y-2)^{2} & =45 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-7632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8fe20d1dde83b850c6707a517b045a868862fa76d815f8b06488be51ce798b91"} {"question" "q-7632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-7632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,2)"] ["math" "(2,1)"] ["math" "(2,4)"] ["math" "(3,5)"] ["math" "(5,3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-7632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-115314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is $3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+36x+32y=-124.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-115314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+36x+32y & =-124 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+36x)+(4{y}^{2}+32y) & =-124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}+36x)+(4{y}^{2}+32y) & =-124 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+12x]+4[{y}^{2}+8y] & =-124 \\\\ 3[(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}]+4[(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-124 \\\\ 3[(x+6)^{2}-36]+4[(y+4)^{2}-16] & =-124 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}-108+4(y+4)^{2}-64 & =-124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+6)^{2}-108+4(y+4)^{2}-64 & =-124 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}+4(y+4)^{2} & =-124+108+64 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}+4(y+4)^{2} & =48 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(-6,-4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-115314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8fe20d1dde83b850c6707a517b045a868862fa76d815f8b06488be51ce798b91"} {"question" "q-115314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-115314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-4,-6)"] ["math" "(-6,-4)"] ["math" "(4,2\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(4,6)"] ["math" "(6,4)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-115314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-6,-4)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-7639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y=-11.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Its center is $(1,-3)$\n- The length of the major axis  is $2\\sqrt{2}$\n- The length of the minor axis is $4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-7639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ and $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y & =-11 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11 \\\\ [{x}^{2}-2x]+2[{y}^{2}+6y] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+2[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-1]+2[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =-11+1+18 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{2}>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(1,-3).$\n\nTherefore,  I and III are true, and II is false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-7639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8fe20d1dde83b850c6707a517b045a868862fa76d815f8b06488be51ce798b91"} {"question" "q-7639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-7639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-7639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-16x+18y=119.$\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-16x+18y & =119 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-16x)+(9{y}^{2}+18y) & =119\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-16x)+(9{y}^{2}+18y) & =119 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-4x)+9({y}^{2}+2y) & =119 \\\\ 4[(x-2)^{2}-4]+9[(y+1)^{2}-1] & =119 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}-16+9(y+1)^{2}-9 & =119\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-2)^{2}-16+9(y+1)^{2}-9 & =119 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}+9(y+1)^{2} & =119+16+9 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}+9(y+1)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6>4,$ the length of the major axis  $=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the length of the minor axis  $=2\\cdot 4=8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8fe20d1dde83b850c6707a517b045a868862fa76d815f8b06488be51ce798b91"} {"question" "q-279720" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279720" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279720" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-279720" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-115316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is\n$2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+16x-2y+29=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-115316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+16x-2y+29 & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+16x)+({y}^{2}-2y)+29 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}+16x)+({y}^{2}-2y)+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+8x]+[{y}^{2}-2y]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+[(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x+4)^{2}-16]+[(y-1)^{2}-1]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}-32+(y-1)^{2}-1+29 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)^{2}-32+(y-1)^{2}-1+29 & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =32+1-29 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-115316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8fe20d1dde83b850c6707a517b045a868862fa76d815f8b06488be51ce798b91"} {"question" "q-115316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-115316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,4)"] ["math" "(-4,1)"] ["math" "(1,2)"] ["math" "(1,4)"] ["math" "(4,-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-115316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-4,1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the length of the major axis of the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+10x-16y+25=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+10x-16y+25 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+10x)+(4{y}^{2}-16y)+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+10x)+(4{y}^{2}-16y)+25 & =0 \\\\ [{x}^{2}+10x]+4[{y}^{2}-4y]+25 & =0 \\\\ [(x+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]+4[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+25 & =0 \\\\ [(x+5)^{2}-25]+4[(y-2)^{2}-4]+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-25+4(y-2)^{2}-16+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-25+4(y-2)^{2}-16+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =25-25+16 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 4=8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8fe20d1dde83b850c6707a517b045a868862fa76d815f8b06488be51ce798b91"} {"question" "q-16565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse whose equation is $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}–48x+72y+144=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Its center is $(6,-4)$\n- The length of the major axis is $6$\n- The length of the minor axis is $4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}–48x+72y+144 & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-48x)+(9{y}^{2}+72y)+144 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-48x)+(9{y}^{2}+72y)+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-12x]+9[{y}^{2}+8y]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}]+9[(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-6)^{2}-36]+9[(y+4)^{2}-16]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}-144+9(y+4)^{2}-144+144 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-6)^{2}-144+9(y+4)^{2}-144+144 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}+9(y+4)^{2} & =-144+144+144 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}+9(y+4)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6>4,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 6=12,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(6,-4).$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, statements II and III are false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8fe20d1dde83b850c6707a517b045a868862fa76d815f8b06488be51ce798b91"} {"question" "q-16562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the length of the minor axis of the ellipse whose equation is  $25{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+100x–40y+100=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+100x–40y+100 & =0 \\\\ (25{x}^{2}+100x)+(4{y}^{2}-40y)+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25{x}^{2}+100x)+(4{y}^{2}-40y)+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[{x}^{2}+4x]+4[{y}^{2}-10y]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+4[(y-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[(x+2)^{2}-4]+4[(y-5)^{2}-25]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}-100+4(y-5)^{2}-100+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25(x+2)^{2}-100+4(y-5)^{2}-100+100 & =0 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}+4(y-5)^{2} & =-100+100+100 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}+4(y-5)^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5>2,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8fe20d1dde83b850c6707a517b045a868862fa76d815f8b06488be51ce798b91"} {"question" "q-16561" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16561" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16561" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse whose equation is $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-9=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Its center is $(0,0)$\n- The length of the major axis is $3$\n- The length of the minor axis is $2\\sqrt{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3>\\sqrt{3},$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 3=6,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the ellipse is centered at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, statement II is false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8fe20d1dde83b850c6707a517b045a868862fa76d815f8b06488be51ce798b91"} {"question" "q-16639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "q-16639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/state.json" "file_sha256" "f492e063e8d980e2b9ef4179483ab35a0a1a7c83b33b97688f7db5aa7876fac4"} {"question" "e-5216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $25x^2 + 9y^2 - 100x - 18y = 116.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/example-5216.json" "file_sha256" "23ead2c6e91cb663559504e1374022fad0cf7aa260514aba43480288bf284aaf"} {"question" "e-5216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-100x-18y & =116 \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-100x)+(9{y}^{2}-18y) & =116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25{x}^{2}-100x)+(9{y}^{2}-18y) & =116 \\\\ 25[{x}^{2}-4x]+9[{y}^{2}-2y] & =116 \\\\ 25[(x-2)^{2}-4]+9[(y-1)^{2}-1] & =116 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}-100+9(y-1)^{2}-9 & =116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25(x-2)^{2}-100+9(y-1)^{2}-9 & =116 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}+9(y-1)^{2} & =116+100+9 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}+9(y-1)^{2} & =225 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5 > 3,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 5 =10,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 3 =6,$ and the ellipse is centered at $({2},{1}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/example-5216.json" "file_sha256" "23ead2c6e91cb663559504e1374022fad0cf7aa260514aba43480288bf284aaf"} {"question" "e-5217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $x^2 + 4y^2 - 6x + 24y + 41 = 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/example-5217.json" "file_sha256" "0a86aa57b958796889d364e415f112e88d328f027ff89a3820706b5579aed0a8"} {"question" "e-5217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-6x+24y+41 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+(4{y}^{2}+24y)+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-6x)+(4{y}^{2}+24y)+41 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+4[{y}^{2}+6y]+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4[(y+3)^{2}-9]+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4(y+3)^{2}-36+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}-9+4(y+3)^{2}-36+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4(y+3)^{2} & =9+36-41 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4(y+3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2 =4,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 1 =2,$ and the ellipse is centered at $({3},{-3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050/example-5217.json" "file_sha256" "0a86aa57b958796889d364e415f112e88d328f027ff89a3820706b5579aed0a8"}] "usage" {"e-5217" [] "e-5216" []} "basis" 1752 "retractions" [[17592186087462 :question/problem "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $25x^2 + 9y^2 - 100x - 18y = 116$."] [17592186087462 :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x^{2} + 9y^{2} - 100x - 18y &= 116 \\\\\n(25x^{2} - 100x) + (9y^{2} - 18y) &= 116\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(25x^{2} - 100x) + (9y^{2} - 18y) &= 116 \\\\\n25[x^{2} - 4x] + 9[y^{2} - 2y] &= 116 \\\\\n25[(x - 2)^{2} - 4] + 9[(y - 1)^{2} - 1] &= 116 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} - 100 + 9(y - 1)^{2} - 9 &= 116\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25(x - 2)^{2} - 100 + 9(y - 1)^{2} - 9 &= 116 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} + 9(y - 1)^{2} &= 116 + 100 + 9 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} + 9(y - 1)^{2} &= 225 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(9) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(25) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(3^{2}) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(5^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5 > 3$, the length of the major axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 5 =10\n$$\n\nthe length of the minor axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3 =6\n$$\n\nand the ellipse is centered at $({2},{1})$."] [17592186087463 :question/problem "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $x^2 + 4y^2 - 6x + 24y + 41 = 0$."] [17592186087463 :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 4y^{2} - 6x + 24y + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6x) + (4y^{2} + 24y) + 41 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} - 6x) + (4y^{2} + 24y) + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6x) + 4[y^{2} + 6y] + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4[(y + 3)^{2} - 9] + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4(y + 3)^{2} - 36 + 41 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4(y + 3)^{2} - 36 + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + 4(y + 3)^{2} &= 9 + 36 - 41 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + 4(y + 3)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n((x - 3)^{2})/(4) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(1) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 3)^{2})/(2^{2}) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(1^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1$, the length of the major axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 2 =4\n$$\n\nthe length of the minor axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1 =2\n$$\n\nand the ellipse is centered at $({3},{-3})$."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
